[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_d3g34")
fish_name = "Starfish"
grade = 3
sprite = ExtResource("2_qalun")
catch_blurb = "At first glance, it seems you’ve caught not a fish from the sea, but a star from the sky."
description = "Without a doubt, the Star-fish is the most magical migrant species to make Pollywood its home. Instead of being native to any body of water, local or otherwise, hundreds of years ago, Star-fish swam down from the swirling sky above Pollywood. Historical records indicate that “schools of stars” were visible circling around the moon for weeks before the fish finally dove, splashing into lakes and rivers all over the planet, and flooding nearby areas. The residents were—and remain—delighted, as not only were the Star-fish beautiful, but the floods aided the explosive growth of nearby wildlife. To this day, the planet prospers because of Star-fish, for around the same time each year, their entire population leaps out of the water and into the sky again for a magnificent migration. Watching their departure has become Pollywood’s most popular tourist attraction, saturating its economy, and the splash of their return hydrates the soil for marshland farmers. By the Pwaaga, the whole phenomenon is taken as proof of Alcedo Atthis’ continued power. Scientists have yet to present a better hypothesis for why they chose Pollywood as their resting place.

In the water, Star-fish tend to be reclusive and keep to their own. However, they’ve been observed to swim at lightning speeds, similar to those of spaceships in hypertravel. This must also be present in their travel through space. They continue to give off light, whether they are in shallow water or the lowest crevices of a deep lake. 

According to Pwaaga myth, Star-fish that fly too high up in the atmosphere freeze into permanent stars to decorate the sky. It’s said that the Nose, Fin, Gill, and Tail stars, the main sources of light and energy for the entire planetary system, were all once huge Star-fish that flew too far from their school. When viewed from above, these stars give the galaxy the outline of a giant fish.
"
metadata/_custom_type_script = "uid://cr3nmjdvpgh0a"
